-# Feature: /shape-plan writes the plan, the epic and the stories
+# Feature: /shape-plan writes the plan, its one epic and one Feature issue per feature
 
 ## What it does
-/shape-plan <intent> runs after /shape-slice, on its own. It reads the slice that serves the intent and the ICE of each feature and its capability, once. It writes the intent's one plan file, one epic for the slice, and one story for each feature. It never writes a failure or recovery condition: the slice's hidden file is /shape-slice's (decision 6 of #844). Built (#818).
+/shape-plan <intent> runs after /design-slice, on its own. It reads the slice that serves the intent and the ICE of each feature and its capability, once. It writes the intent's one plan file, its one epic, and one Feature issue for each feature. It does not file stories (ADR 068, ADR 069). It never writes a failure or recovery condition: the slice's hidden file is /design-slice's (decision 6 of #844). Built (#818).
 
-> To be deepened later: written by /shape-slice at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.
+> To be deepened later: written by /design-slice at the directional depth. /discover-domains or /discover-capabilities raises it to the detailed depth.
 
 ## Inputs / Outputs
 - In: the slice that serves the intent, from the spine.
 - In: the ICE of each feature of the slice and the ICE of that feature's capability, read once.
 - Out: the intent's one plan file (`.garura/project/plans/<intent>.yaml`).
-- Out: one epic for the slice and one story for each feature, in the tracker after the product owner's yes.
-- Out: on the epic, the feature-to-story map: for each feature of the slice, the story that delivers it (ontology v11, Epic). It agrees with the plan's Tree.
-- Thin: the fields of the plan file and of each story are not set yet.
+- Out: the plan's one epic and one Feature issue for each feature, in the tracker after the product owner's yes.
+- Out: on the epic, the feature-to-Feature-issue map: for each feature of the slice, the Feature issue that delivers it (ontology v11, Epic). It agrees with the plan's Tree.
+- Thin: the fields of the plan file and of each Feature issue are not set yet.
 
 ## Rules & behavior
-- The play runs after /shape-slice, on its own, and needs the slice to exist.
-- Each story carries the goal, the limits, the expectations and the Context guidance from its feature's ICE and its capability's ICE.
-- The play never writes a failure or recovery condition. The slice's hidden file belongs to /shape-slice (decision 6).
+- The play runs after /design-slice, on its own, and needs the slice to exist.
+- Each Feature issue carries the goal, the limits, the expectations and the Context guidance from its feature's ICE and its capability's ICE.
+- The play never writes a failure or recovery condition. The slice's hidden file belongs to /design-slice (decision 6).
 - When the intent already has a plan file, the play stops at pre-flight, before it opens a change or writes anything, and points to /update-plan, which changes a plan that exists (#896).
 - Nothing goes to the tracker before the product owner's yes.
-- The story is not the build's whole brief. The builder reads the story and the context that its context sub-agent collects from the story's Context guidance.
-- A story never names the eval, the hidden file or the plan's evals folder. Its Context guidance does not name them either. So the build is given the intent, never the eval (fn-898).
+- The Feature issue is not the build's whole brief. The builder reads the Feature issue and the context that its context sub-agent collects from the Feature issue's Context guidance.
+- A Feature issue never names the eval, the hidden file or the plan's evals folder. Its Context guidance does not name them either. So the build is given the intent, never the eval (fn-898).
 - A slice that changes after the plan exists is handled by /update-plan, not by a second run of /shape-plan (#896).
 
 ## Acceptance criteria
-- Given a slice with three features, when the product owner runs /shape-plan and types yes, then one plan file, one epic and three stories exist.
+- Given a slice with three features, when the product owner runs /shape-plan and types yes, then one plan file, one epic and three Feature issues exist.
 - Given a plan that exists, when the product owner runs /shape-plan, then the play stops before it opens a change or writes anything, and names /update-plan.
-- Given the stories and the plan file, when a person reads them, then none holds a failure or recovery condition.
+- Given the Feature issues and the plan file, when a person reads them, then none holds a failure or recovery condition.
 - Given no yes from the product owner, when the play stops, then nothing is in the tracker.
-- Given a slice with three features, when the product owner runs /shape-plan and types yes, then the epic holds the feature-to-story map: one line for each feature, with the story that delivers it.
+- Given a slice with three features, when the product owner runs /shape-plan and types yes, then the epic holds the feature-to-Feature-issue map: one line for each feature, with the Feature issue that delivers it.
 
 ## Out of scope
-- The slice and its hidden file. /shape-slice writes them (fn-795).
-- The eval. /shape-eval writes it (fn-798).
+- The slice and its hidden file. /design-slice writes them (fn-795).
+- The eval. /design-eval writes it (fn-798).
 - The build, and the design, test or validation of an epic (#819).
 - The checks that no build is given the evals folder and that flag a build session that read it (fn-898).
-- A change to a plan that exists, a later slice's epic and stories included. /update-plan makes it (fn-730).
+- A change to a plan that exists, a later slice's Feature issues included. /update-plan makes it (fn-730).
 
 ## Sources
 - GitHub #818 (closed): the plan play. GitHub #896: /shape-plan stops when the intent already has a plan, and /update-plan changes it.
